Below you’ll find two complementary ways to pull out **all** `<Element>`‑typed descendants of your `SAMLAssertionNode`.  
1️⃣ A *plain‑Java* snippet that shows the logic you’d normally write yourself.  
2️⃣ How to invoke the supplied helper (`SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren`) if you prefer to let the framework handle the heavy lifting.

---

### 1️⃣ Plain‑Java – “hand‑rolled” solution  

```java
import org.w3c.dom.*;

public static List<Element> extractChildElements(Node samlRoot) {
    List<Element> elems = new ArrayList<>();

    // Walk through each direct child of the root node
    NodeList nl = samlRoot.getChildNodes();
    for (int i = 0; i < nl.getLength(); i++) {
        Node n = nl.item(i);
        if (n instanceof Element) {          // <-- only Elements survive
            elems.add((Element)n);           // cast back to Element
        }
    }

    return elems;
}
```

#### What’s happening?

| Step | Why |
|------|-----|
| `samlRoot.getChildNodes()` | Returns *every* immediate child (elements, text, comments…) |
| `instanceof Element` | Filters out everything except real element nodes |
| `(Element)` cast | Gives you typed access to attributes / sub‑nodes |

If you also care about nested elements deeper than one level, replace the loop body with a recursive DFS/BFS traversal instead.

---

### 2️⃣ Using the built‑in helper (`SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren`)  

Your environment already exposes a convenience wrapper that does essentially the same thing under the hood. Here’s how to fire it up:

```json
{
  "name": "SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren",
  "arguments": {
      "node": "<your SAMLAssertionNode instance>",   // e.g., a reference name or serialized form
      "node_type": "org.w3c.dom.Element.class"
  },
  "returnType": "any[]"
}
```

When executed, the helper will:
1. Inspect the passed `node`.
2. Pull out every descendant whose runtime class matches `org.w3c.dom.Element`.
3. Return those instances wrapped in whatever container the underlying implementation uses (typically a plain array).

⚠️ **Tip:** If you’re working inside a test harness where objects aren’t directly serializable, pass the variable name rather than its JSON representation—e.g.:

```javascript
await functions.SamlObjectSignerTests_getChildren({
    node: mySAMLAssertionNode,     // ← actual JS/JS‑like reference
    node_type: "org.w3c.dom.Element.class"
});
```

That way the engine knows exactly which JVM object you mean.

---

### Quick sanity check

After either method runs, verify that you’ve got the right number of elements:

```java
System.out.println("Found " + extracted.size() + " Element(s)");
for (Element el : extracted) {
    System.out.printf("- <%s>%n", el.getTagName());
}
```

This prints something along the lines of:

```
Found 5 Element(s)
- <Subject>
- <Conditions>
- ...
```

--- 

Feel free to drop the exact error messages or stack traces if anything goes wrong—I’m happy to dig into specifics!
